{"accepted_at":"2026-08-30T15:41:20.217737Z","case":{"argument_date":"2025-03-26T00:00:00Z","arguments":[{"argument_date":"2025-03-26T00:00:00Z","heading":"What happened at the argument","official_detail_url":"https://www.supremecourt.gov/oral_arguments/argument_transcript/2024","official_transcript_url":"https://www.supremecourt.gov/oral_arguments/argument_transcripts/2024/24-354_3e07.pdf","paragraphs":["General Harris argued Section 254 is not an unlimited delegation. She stated Congress told the FCC to give all Americans access to basic services. She emphasized the FCC can promote phone service but not faxes.","Mr. Clement argued there is no delegation problem. He stated Congress did not impose a tax out of the blue. He claimed the 1996 Act provided a clear basis for the fees.","Mr. McCotter argued the case is about taxation without representation. He stated Americans pay billions annually for the fund. He noted the rate has increased ten-fold since the law passed."],"reargument":false,"sequence":1,"sources":[{"evidence_type":"Transcript","label":"Official Supreme Court Transcript — file page 10, lines 23-31","official_url":"https://www.supremecourt.gov/oral_arguments/argument_transcripts/2024/24-354_3e07.pdf","page_label":"file page 10, lines 23-31"},{"evidence_type":"Transcript","label":"Official Supreme Court Transcript — file page 106, lines 23-9","official_url":"https://www.supremecourt.gov/oral_arguments/argument_transcripts/2024/24-354_3e07.pdf","page_label":"file page 106, lines 23-9"},{"evidence_type":"Transcript","label":"Official Supreme Court Transcript — file page 11, lines 47-53","official_url":"https://www.supremecourt.gov/oral_arguments/argument_transcripts/2024/24-354_3e07.pdf","page_label":"file page 11, lines 47-53"},{"evidence_type":"Transcript","label":"Official Supreme Court Transcript — file page 112, lines 11-53","official_url":"https://www.supremecourt.gov/oral_arguments/argument_transcripts/2024/24-354_3e07.pdf","page_label":"file page 1